# React Patterns

React 19 in a plain SPA inside a `BrowserWindow`. There is no router-based framework here: no App Router, no Server Components, no `next/*` imports, no route builders. Renderer layer definitions are in [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md); this file is how components are written.

## Separate Logic From Presentation

> A component file describes **what the UI looks like**. All of **how it behaves** lives in a colocated `use<ComponentName>` hook.

- The component takes `props`, calls its hook once, destructures, and renders.
- The hook owns state, data fetching, handlers, derived values, and effects, and returns a flat object of ready-to-render values and callbacks.
- Handlers live in the hook (`onApproveClick`, `onSelectionPrompt`, `onDismissRun`), never inline in JSX.
- Colocate the pair:

  ```
  modules/review/DiffReview/
  ├── DiffReview.tsx        # presentational: props in, JSX out
  ├── useDiffReview.ts      # state, handlers, derived values, debounced write-back
  └── components/           # child components, same pattern
  ```

- Don't over-apply: a tiny purely-presentational component (`StateBadge`) needs no hook — adding one is pure indirection.

```tsx
// ✅
export function DiffReview({ run }: DiffReviewProps) {
  const { editorValue, changedFiles, isRevising, onEditorChange, onApproveClick } =
    useDiffReview(run);
  // …JSX only
}

// ❌ fetching, debouncing and diffing inside the component body
export function DiffReview({ run }: DiffReviewProps) {
  const { data } = useQuery(/* … */);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => writeBack(value), 300);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value]);
}
```

## No Logic in JSX

No state reads, comparisons, computations, `.filter`/`.reduce`, or string building inside returned markup. Compute in the hook, name the result, render the name.

```tsx
// ❌
<span>{formatDuration(Date.now() - run.startedAt)}</span>
<p>{runs.filter((r) => r.state === RUN_STATE.READY).length} ready</p>

// ✅ elapsedLabel and readyCount come from the hook
<span>{elapsedLabel}</span>
<p>{readyCount} ready</p>
```

## No Conditional Rendering in JSX

No inline ternaries and no `&&` in returned markup. Assign to a named `const` first.

```tsx
// ✅
const content = isReady ? <DiffReview run={run} /> : <TranscriptStream run={run} />;
const guardrailFlags = hasFlags ? <GuardrailFlags flags={flags} /> : null;
return (
  <div>
    {guardrailFlags}
    {content}
  </div>
);

// ❌
return <div>{hasFlags && <GuardrailFlags flags={flags} />}{isReady ? <DiffReview /> : <Transcript />}</div>;
```

This rule has unusual bite here: the Workspace right-hand pane branches on `RunState`. That branch is a named `const` computed in a hook — never a ternary chain in JSX.

## IIFE Over Nested Ternaries

When a value depends on several conditions, use an IIFE with early returns.

```tsx
const emptyStateLabel = (() => {                            // ✅
  if (!selectedComment) return 'Select a comment to begin';
  if (isPrCommentsLoading) return 'Loading comments…';
  return 'No run yet — press a to start one';
})();
```

## `switch` + `assertNever` Over Discriminated Unions

When exhausting `RunState` or `PrComment['kind']`, use `switch` with `assertNever` in the default so a new variant becomes a compile error rather than a blank pane.

```tsx
// ✅ in usePaneContent.ts
const pane = (() => {
  switch (run.state) {
    case RUN_STATE.QUEUED:
    case RUN_STATE.RUNNING:
      return <TranscriptStream run={run} />;
    case RUN_STATE.NEEDS_DECISION:
      return <DecisionPrompt run={run} />;
    case RUN_STATE.REVISING:
    case RUN_STATE.READY:
    case RUN_STATE.APPROVED:
    case RUN_STATE.APPLIED:
      return <DiffReview run={run} />;
    case RUN_STATE.NO_ACTION_NEEDED:
    case RUN_STATE.FAILED:
      return <RunOutcome run={run} />;
    default:
      return assertNever(run.state);
  }
})();

// ❌ a new state silently renders nothing
const pane = run.state === RUN_STATE.READY ? <DiffReview /> : <TranscriptStream />;
```

## Component API

- **No `forwardRef`.** React 19 passes `ref` as a regular prop — declare it in the props interface.
- Named exports, one component per file, `function` declarations. No `React.FC`, no default exports.
- Explicit props interfaces (see `typescript-standards.mdc`). Never derive props with `typeof`.
- Variation on shared components goes through `variant` / `size` props, never per-instance `className` overrides.
- No `useEffect` for anything derivable during render; effects are for subscriptions (IPC event streams) and imperative Monaco wiring.

```tsx
// ✅ React 19
interface Props {
  ref?: React.Ref<HTMLButtonElement>;
  variant: ButtonVariant;
}

// ❌
const Button = forwardRef<HTMLButtonElement, Props>((props, ref) => { /* … */ });
```

## No Inline SVG

Every SVG becomes a component in `src/renderer/src/components/icons/`. Icons take `className` for sizing and colour and never hard-code a fill colour.

```tsx
import { CheckIcon } from '@renderer/components/icons/CheckIcon';  // ✅
<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="…" /></svg>                      // ❌
```

## No Barrel Exports

No `index.ts` re-exporting siblings. Import directly from the defining file — barrels defeat tree-shaking, hide cycles, and make the module boundary lint zones unenforceable.

`src/main/ipc.ts` and `src/shared/comments.ts` are **not** barrels; they define contracts and models rather than re-exporting neighbours.

## File Organization

Renderer layout is `src/renderer/src/{screens,modules,components,hooks,stores,lib}`:

- `screens/` — pages (`Workspace`, `Settings`), each owning its page-focused components and its own orchestration.
- `modules/` — cross-page feature blocks (`discovery`, `comments`, `review`, `runs`, `landing`, `audit`, `settings`), each owning its components, their colocated hooks, its data hooks, and module-local types.
- `components/` — proven shared primitives, plus `icons/`. `hooks/` — cross-module hooks only. `stores/` — Zustand, one domain per store. `lib/` — `queryKeys`, `classNames`, `formatDuration`, `assertNever`, …

Four boundary rules; the first two are ESLint errors, not review nitpicks:

1. **Pages never import pages.** `Workspace` cannot reach into `Settings` or vice versa. A page-focused component a second page wants has stopped being page-focused — move it into `modules/`.
2. **Modules may compose each other, but import cycles are a lint error.** `landing` reusing the diff viewer and guardrail flags is the point of the module layer; two modules importing each other is one undeletable blob.
3. **Modules never import from `screens/`.** Direction is strictly downward: screens → modules → `components/`/`hooks/`/`stores/`/`lib/`.
4. **Born scoped, promoted on the second consumer.** Start in the most local layer that works. Promote page → module when a second page needs it, module → `components/` when it proves to be a generic primitive (`TreeRow` and `StateBadge` qualify because the comment tree and the diff's file tree both render them). Never promote speculatively.

Cross-page data flows through props or shared state, never a page import: the selected comment is lifted to `Workspace`; run state is read from `stores/runStore`.

## Accessibility

- Every interactive element has an accessible label (visible text or `aria-label`) and a **visible focus ring**. Never `outline-none` without a replacement.
- **Every keyboard shortcut has a visible button equivalent.** `j`/`k`, `a`, `r`, `e`, `]`/`[`, `d`, `Cmd+K` are accelerators, never the only path — and `?` shows the map.
- Landing stays a deliberate two-step: a shortcut may open the preview, confirmation is a click. The gate must not become muscle memory.
- Use real semantics: `button` for actions, `role="tree"`/`treeitem` with `aria-expanded` on the comment tree, `aria-live` for streamed run-state changes.
- Never convey state by colour alone — `StateBadge` carries text.
- Keep transitions consistent across interactive elements; respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

User-facing strings are hard-coded English. There is no i18n layer in this project and no translation keys.
